Windows release review corrections — native icon validator hardening

Scope and gate
- Branch: codex/windows-installed-brand-validation
- Corrects the review findings introduced around 86f5a8c2; application runtime, Tauri, NSIS hooks, icon assets, macOS, and Linux behavior are unchanged.
- v1.1.5 remains blocked until this commit passes a new full PR CI and a new independent review.
- The rendered-pixel comparison remains intentionally in place. Direct PE/ICO resource canonicalization from review point 9 was not added without a proven need; both sides now use one native extractor and required source frames are checked independently.

Root causes verified before correction
- PrivateExtractIcons returns the requested output dimensions even for 31, 37, and 200 pixels, so the old 32x32 equality check was tautological.
- Drawing.Icon.ExtractAssociatedIcon returned a generic 40x40 shell icon for C:\Windows\System32\where.exe although PrivateExtractIcons found no embedded icon resource.
- PowerShell relative-path lookup and IO.Path.GetFullPath diverged when the PowerShell location and process current directory differed.
- PR CI exercised only pwsh while release CI also exercised Windows PowerShell 5.1.
- The prior automated test extracted only icon.ico and never reached the icon path of a real executable.

Architecture and decisions
- scripts/release/windows-package-file.ps1 is the single package-file boundary: one PowerShell-relative resolution, regular-file/reparse/size validation, and generic failures.
- scripts/release/windows-native-icon.ps1 is the single System.Drawing/native interop owner. The embedded C# class name is derived from a SHA-256 of its source, so edited interop recompiles in long-lived PowerShell sessions.
- Native handles are cloned into managed icons and released on every path. Extraction failures and runtime/cleanup failures are distinct.
- scripts/release/windows-icon-validation.ps1 owns ICO structure parsing, the required Windows frame sizes (32, 48, and 256), two-background pixel hashing, and multi-size EXE/ICO comparison.
- The source installer check now uses the native resource extractor and cannot accept Windows' generic fallback icon.
- Cleanup/environment failures map to source-installer-icon-runtime or installed-brand-icon-runtime instead of blaming the reference ICO.
- The dead installed-brand-icon-size category and null check were removed.
- scripts/test-install-ps1.ps1 discovers every release/*.ps1 file, so new validators cannot silently escape syntax parsing.
- package-migration.test.mjs parses CI YAML structurally for the pwsh/PowerShell 5.1 pair and removes icon implementation-shape assertions now covered by execution.

